(function(){try{
(()=>{'use strict';
const pages={
"/destination-recommendations":`<section id="sr-hub-editorial" class="wrap section"><p class="kicker">BUILD A BIGGER DAY OUT</p><h2>Use destination recommendations as a planning layer, not a random list</h2><p>The recommendations section connects the arcade directory with the rest of a destination. A useful day out often includes more than one stop: an arcade visit might sit alongside a museum, seafront walk, shopping area, family attraction, live venue, restaurant or overnight stay. The purpose of these cards is to give visitors realistic options around the places already covered by Spin Raiders.</p><p>Recommendations are kept separate from venue facts. Being listed nearby does not mean a business is included with an arcade visit, owned by the same operator or covered by the same opening hours. Check the individual attraction or accommodation provider before travelling, especially where booking, parking or seasonal opening matters.</p><p>Start with the town or resort you are visiting, choose the arcade or gaming venue that fits your group, then use the surrounding recommendations to fill the rest of the day. This is particularly useful in seaside destinations where several arcades, food stops and attractions can be visited on foot, and in larger cities where travel time between districts needs more planning.</p></section>`,
"/classic-fruit-machines":`<section id="sr-hub-editorial" class="wrap section"><p class="kicker">PHYSICAL MACHINE ARCHIVE</p><h2>How the Spin Raiders classic fruit-machine archive is being documented</h2><p>The archive is built around physical fruit machines rather than generic game names. A title can exist in different cabinets, stake-and-prize packages, board revisions or later conversions, so Spin Raiders keeps separate records where there is evidence of a meaningful physical or technical difference. That is why identifying a machine from a photograph involves more than reading the logo.</p><p>Useful evidence includes the manufacturer name, cabinet artwork, reel window, button deck, stake and jackpot markings, board or technology family, printed flyers, manuals and photographs of surviving examples. Where a release year, stake, jackpot or cabinet has not been verified, the archive now says so explicitly rather than filling gaps from memory or copying a similar machine. This is especially important with machines that were re-glassed, re-staked or converted during their working lives.</p><p>Open an individual record to see the information currently held for that title. Some pages have extensive history and technical notes; others remain evidence-led identification records while stronger material is gathered. Photographs are intended to show the real machine or a verified archive image, with variants kept separate where possible. Flyers and external references can help identify artwork or specifications but are not a substitute for a real-machine photograph when one is available.</p><p>The archive is designed for collectors, arcade visitors and anyone trying to put a name to a machine they remember. If you have stronger physical evidence for a record, the best contribution is a clear photograph or source that can be tied to that exact version.</p></section>`,
"/blog":`<section id="sr-hub-editorial" class="wrap section"><p class="kicker">SPIN RAIDERS ARTICLES</p><h2>Arcade guides, classic-machine history and practical trip planning</h2><p>The Spin Raiders blog expands on information that does not fit neatly into a directory card. Articles cover arcade visits, classic fruit machines, destination planning, gaming-industry changes and practical guides for people deciding where to travel or what to look for when they arrive.</p><p>Directory pages answer the immediate questions about a place; articles can go deeper into a machine family, a route around a resort, changes seen on repeat visits or the background behind a particular type of arcade. Older posts should be read with their publication date in mind because machine line-ups, ownership and opening arrangements can change.</p><p>Use the category links to move between destination guides, UK arcade coverage, fruit machines and retro/classic content. When an article discusses a specific current venue, follow through to the directory page for the latest structured visitor information.</p></section>`,
"/attractions":`<section id="sr-hub-editorial" class="wrap section"><p class="kicker">NEARBY ATTRACTIONS</p><h2>Turn an arcade stop into a fuller trip</h2><p>Nearby attractions are included to solve a practical planning problem: not everybody in a group wants to spend the whole day in an arcade. The directory therefore records other places that may fit the same trip, from museums and family attractions to bowling, live entertainment, shopping and other local activities.</p><p>Proximity does not imply a partnership, shared ticket or guaranteed opening time. Treat each suggestion as an independent stop and check its own website or booking information before travelling. Where a destination has several possible attractions, choose them around the age of the group, travel time, weather and how long you expect to spend at the main venue.</p><p>For coastal resorts this can mean combining the promenade and arcades with a pier, beach or indoor attraction. In cities it may mean planning around public transport and different districts. The aim is to make the venue directory useful for a complete day rather than a single pin on a map.</p></section>`,
"/locations":`<section id="sr-hub-editorial" class="wrap section"><p class="kicker">UK DESTINATIONS</p><h2>Plan arcade trips by town, resort and city</h2><p>The location directory groups venue records into destinations so visitors can see the wider arcade scene rather than opening isolated listings one at a time. A destination page can bring together traditional arcades, adult gaming venues, bowling, holiday parks and other relevant stops while also giving practical context about the area.</p><p>Use destination pages when the trip matters as much as the individual venue. Seaside resorts may support several arcade stops within walking distance, while larger towns and cities often require more travel between venues. Parking, public transport, accessibility and nearby attractions are therefore part of the planning picture.</p><p>Coverage varies by destination. Spin Raiders distinguishes public-ready records from places still being researched, so a smaller list does not necessarily mean no other venues exist. Report missing or changed locations when you have current evidence that can be checked.</p></section>`,
"/destinations":`<section id="sr-hub-editorial" class="wrap section"><p class="kicker">UK DESTINATIONS</p><h2>Choose the place first, then build the arcade route</h2><p>Destination browsing is useful when you know the town or resort you want to visit but have not decided which arcade or attraction should anchor the day. Spin Raiders combines venue records with practical local information so visitors can compare different stops, understand the character of the area and plan realistic travel between them.</p><p>Machine sightings remain venue-specific and date-sensitive. A destination may be known for a strong arcade scene without every machine being available on every visit. Open the individual venue guides for current evidence, then use the destination page for the surrounding trip.</p></section>`,
"/casinos":`<section id="sr-hub-editorial" class="wrap section"><p class="kicker">UK LAND-BASED CASINOS</p><h2>Physical casino venues kept separate from online offers</h2><p>This section covers land-based casino venues and their visitor context. It is separate from the online casino-offers area so a person looking for a physical venue is not presented with an affiliate offer as though it were the same product. Casino entry is for adults and individual venues have their own identification, membership, dress, game and opening arrangements.</p><p>Use the venue information to check the operator, location and any verified facilities, then confirm current entry requirements directly with the casino before travelling. Games, tables, poker schedules, food service and promotions can change by venue and date.</p></section>`
};
function mount(){
 const p=(location.pathname||'/').replace(/\/$/,'')||'/';const html=pages[p];
 if(!html||document.getElementById('sr-hub-editorial'))return;
 const root=document.getElementById('sr-seaside-root')||document.getElementById('PAGES_CONTAINER')||document.body;
 const main=root.querySelector('main')||root,foot=root.querySelector('footer');
 const box=document.createElement('div');box.innerHTML=html;const node=box.firstElementChild;
 foot?foot.before(node):main.append(node);
}
let n=0;const t=setInterval(()=>{mount();if(document.getElementById('sr-hub-editorial')||++n>50)clearInterval(t)},100);
addEventListener('pageshow',mount);addEventListener('popstate',()=>setTimeout(mount,100));
})();
}catch(e){console.warn('SR snippet failed: Spin Raiders Hub Editorial Depth 20260918',e)}})();

